6440816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ec5d3c3-0881-5afc-b51d-bb796f5a2561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440816 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/a0/a0e43112312f6fc14ec22d8fcefd13046c5f8be2fb980904b3f6a236a27dc9d2.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440817 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-3)^{2}=3(x-5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8beb7550-388c-58d2-8337-e23626a88235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440817 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}=4(\\frac{3}{4})(x-5).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=5$ and $k=3.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(5,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440818 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/87/87f6b5b68c7447d154926023fae8f0215ea927d122f42e375b11d00e88a520e6.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c70a41e9-d1c3-58ec-ac14-5fc542b5be6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440818 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/b9/b9def5f9de72d5b739041cecf5d1cba3dd09a7c5fefc1001431b05f9820049b2.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(6,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440819 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440819 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440819 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440819 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=3x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eaf2fda4-efdd-5da9-b4eb-598434fc7b28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186440819 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(\\frac{3}{4})x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
